# AGENTS.md

Global development guidelines for the Bog Agents monorepo. Loaded into every session as persistent context.

## Project Overview

Bog Agents is a production-ready AI agent framework built on LangGraph (MIT). Three published packages:

- **`bog-agents`** (`libs/bog-agents/`) — Python SDK. `create_agent()` returns a compiled LangGraph agent with filesystem tools, shell, subagents, plan mode, retry-with-backoff, and 80+ composable middlewares.
- **`bog-agents-cli`** (`libs/cli/`) — Terminal coding agent (Textual TUI). 80+ slash commands, any LLM provider, persistent memory, MCP marketplace, `/peat` scheduler, `/qa` harness, `/record`+`/replay`, in-memory secrets vault.
- **`bog-agents-daemon`** (`libs/daemon/`) — Ambient runner. Executes agents on cron/file-change/webhook/git-push triggers; reports via Slack/email/GitHub/webhook.

**Tech stack:** Python 3.11+, LangGraph, LangChain, Textual (TUI), uv (package manager), ruff (lint/format), ty (type checker), pytest.

## Architecture

```txt
bog-agents/
├── libs/
│   ├── bog-agents/       # Core SDK — create_agent(), middleware stack, backends
│   │   └── bog_agents/
│   │       ├── graph.py          # create_agent() entry point; wires middleware + LangGraph
│   │       ├── builder.py        # AgentBuilder / AgentConfig fluent API
│   │       ├── feature_config.py # FeatureConfig — feature flags for middleware
│   │       ├── middleware/       # 80+ AgentMiddleware subclasses (lazy-loaded)
│   │       ├── backends/         # Pluggable filesystem, shell, state, sandbox backends
│   │       ├── sandbox/          # Sandbox execution support
│   │       └── utils/            # Shared utilities
│   ├── cli/              # bog-agents-cli — Textual TUI
│   │   └── bog_agents_cli/
│   │       ├── main.py           # CLI entry point / argparse
│   │       ├── app.py            # BogAgentsApp (Textual Application)
│   │       ├── agent.py          # Agent wiring for the CLI
│   │       ├── command_registry.py # SlashCommandSpec definitions (source of truth)
│   │       ├── model_config.py   # PROVIDER_API_KEY_ENV mapping
│   │       ├── config.py         # Settings / detect_provider()
│   │       ├── ui.py             # show_help() — hand-maintained help screen
│   │       └── widgets/          # Textual widgets
│   ├── daemon/           # bog-agents-daemon — ambient trigger runner
│   ├── acp/              # Agent Client Protocol (Zed editor integration)
│   ├── harbor/           # Evaluation / benchmark framework (Terminal Bench 2.0)
│   ├── vscode-extension/ # VS Code extension
│   └── partners/         # Sandbox integrations (daytona, runloop)
├── .github/              # CI/CD workflows, PR lint, release-please
├── scripts/              # repo.ps1 bootstrap / helpers
└── Makefile              # Root-level lint/format/lock across all packages
```

**SDK middleware pattern:** All middleware inherits from `AgentMiddleware` and implements `wrap_model_call()`. Middleware is lazy-loaded via `_LAZY_IMPORTS` in `bog_agents/__init__.py` — follow this pattern when adding new middleware. Wiring lives in `graph.py`.

**Backends:** Swappable implementations for filesystem (`local`, `composite`, `sandbox`), shell execution, and state storage.

## Development

### Setup

```bash
# Prerequisites: Python 3.11+, uv, git

# Clone
git clone https://github.com/bogware/bog-agents.git
cd bog-agents

# Windows PowerShell bootstrap
./scripts/repo.ps1 init

# Per-package install (each package is independent)
cd libs/bog-agents && uv sync
cd libs/cli && uv sync       # links to local SDK via uv.sources

# Run CLI locally
cd libs/cli && uv run bog-agents
```

### Common Commands

Run from the relevant package directory (`libs/bog-agents/`, `libs/cli/`, etc.):

```bash
make test              # Unit tests (no network, parallel via pytest-xdist)
make integration_test  # Integration tests (network allowed)
make lint              # ruff check + ruff format --diff + ty type check
make format            # ruff format + ruff check --fix
make coverage          # Unit tests with XML + terminal coverage report
make test_watch        # Watch mode (ptw)
make benchmark         # Benchmark tests
```

From repo root (runs across all packages):

```bash
make lint              # Lint all packages
make format            # Format all packages
make lock              # Update all uv.lock files
make lock-check        # Verify all lockfiles are up-to-date
```

Single test file:

```bash
uv run --group test pytest tests/unit_tests/test_specific.py
```

SDK type check only:

```bash
uv run --all-groups ty check bog_agents   # from libs/bog-agents/
```

### Testing

- Framework: `pytest` with `pytest-asyncio` (`asyncio_mode = "auto"` in every `pyproject.toml`)
- **Do NOT add `@pytest.mark.asyncio`** — it's automatic
- Unit tests: `tests/unit_tests/` — no network calls (`--disable-socket`)
- Integration tests: `tests/integration_tests/` — network allowed
- Test file structure mirrors source structure
- Avoid mocks; test actual implementation
- Tests must be deterministic (no flaky tests)
- Every new feature/bugfix requires unit test coverage

## Code Conventions

- **Language:** Python 3.11+, mandatory type hints on all public functions, no `Any` in public APIs
- **Line length:** 150 characters (ruff)
- **Formatter/linter:** ruff (both lint and format)
- **Type checker:** ty (`uv run --all-groups ty check`)
- **Docstrings:** Google-style with `Args:`, `Returns:`, `Raises:` — types in signatures, NOT in docstrings
- **Inline code:** Single backticks only — never Sphinx double-backtick (` ``code`` `)
- **Spelling:** American English (e.g., "behavior", not "behaviour")
- **Ruff suppression:** Inline `# noqa: RULE` for individual exceptions; `per-file-ignores` only for categorical policy (e.g., all tests)
- **Async tests:** No `@pytest.mark.asyncio` decorator needed
- **Error handling:** No bare `except:`; use `msg` variable for error messages
- **Security:** No `eval()`/`exec()`/`pickle` on user-controlled input; proper resource cleanup
- **Imports (CLI):** Never import heavy packages at module level in entry points — defer to call site

**Public API stability:** Preserve function signatures. Use keyword-only args for new params: `*, new_param: str = "default"`. Check `__init__.py` exports before modifying public interfaces.

**Commit format (Conventional Commits, lowercase, scope required):**

```
feat(sdk): add new chat completion feature
fix(cli): resolve type hinting issue
chore(harbor): update infrastructure dependencies
```

Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`. `feat` → minor bump, `fix` → patch bump.

